    <!-- 1.2 Execution Paradigms: Scalable RVV vs 2D Systolic Matrix -->
    <h3 class="subsection-title">1.2 Execution Paradigms: Scalable RVV 1.0 vs 2D Spatial Systolic Arrays</h3>
    <p class="sub-lead">
      Accelerating high-density linear algebra divides open-source silicon into two fundamentally different execution philosophies:
    </p>

    <div class="paradigm-compare-grid">
      <div class="paradigm-col">
        <div class="paradigm-header bg-yellow">
          <span class="p-badge">1D DYNAMIC VECTORIZATION</span>
          <h4>RISC-V Vector 1.0 (RVV) Coprocessors</h4>
          <span class="p-sub">Ara, AraXL, Spatz, Vicuna</span>
        </div>
        <div class="paradigm-body">
          <p>
            <strong>Vector-Length Agnostic (VLA) Programming:</strong> Binaries compile once and run unmodified across any hardware vector length (\(VLEN \in [128, 4096]\)) and lane count (\(1 \le N \le 16\)). The application configures <code>vl</code> via <code>vsetvli</code> and loops through arbitrary tensor dimensions without recompilation.
          </p>
          <ul class="paradigm-points">
            <li><strong>Strengths:</strong> Extreme mathematical flexibility—executes non-linear activation functions (Softmax, GELU, Exp), FFT permutations, arbitrary 1D/2D convolutions, and general scientific computing.</li>
            <li><strong>Limitations:</strong> Register file and crossbar area scale aggressively. High-density GEMM requires continuous register streaming, leaving utilization vulnerable to scalar host issue bubbles.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="paradigm-col">
        <div class="paradigm-header bg-teal">
          <span class="p-badge">2D SPATIAL SYSTOLIC ACCELERATION</span>
          <h4>2D Spatial Systolic Arrays</h4>
          <span class="p-sub">Gemmini (UC Berkeley / Chipyard)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="paradigm-body">
          <p>
            <strong>Rigid Tiled Spatial Dataflow:</strong> Processing elements (PEs) are arranged in a 2D mesh (\(16 	imes 16\) or \(32 	imes 32\)). Data streams rhythmically through neighboring PEs in a weight-stationary or output-stationary pattern, bypassing register files entirely during the inner dot-product accumulation.
          </p>
          <ul class="paradigm-points">
            <li><strong>Strengths:</strong> Peerless arithmetic density. Yields 256 to 1024 MACs per cycle with minimal control logic overhead and orders-of-magnitude lower register file read energy.</li>
            <li><strong>Limitations:</strong> Highly rigid dataflow. Kernels that cannot be framed as dense 2D matrix multiplications (such as sparse graphs or non-linear reductions) achieve near-zero utilization.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 1.3 The Arithmetic Density Triad (Top-Down Progressive Flow) -->
    <h3 class="subsection-title" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">1.3 The Arithmetic Density Triad (Top-Down Progressive Flow)</h3>
    <p class="sub-lead">
      Silicon architects navigate three interdependent design axes when sizing vector accelerators, trading silicon gate budgets against memory bandwidth and dataflow specialization:
    </p>

    <div class="triad-vertical-flow">
      <!-- Axis 1 -->
      <div class="triad-card bg-cream">
        <div class="triad-head">
          <span class="t-badge badge-green">AXIS 1 &bull; COMPACT EMBEDDED</span>
          <h4 class="t-title">Lane Count &amp; Datapath Width (Spatz / Vicuna ~40 kGE)</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="t-desc">
          Single-lane embedded vector coprocessor coupled to lightweight microcontrollers (Snitch, CV32E40P, Ibex). Eliminates crossbar routing entirely; single-port latch VRF provides 1–2 FLOPS/cycle at minimal dynamic energy, ideal for IoT edge inference.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 1 -> 2 -->
      <div class="flow-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Scale SIMD Datapath to Standard RVV 1.0 Multi-Lane (+180 kGE Silicon Investment)</span>
      </div>

      <!-- Axis 2 -->
      <div class="triad-card bg-light-blue">
        <div class="triad-head">
          <span class="t-badge badge-blue">AXIS 2 &bull; SCALABLE RVV 1.0</span>
          <h4 class="t-title">VRF Multi-Banking &amp; Crossbar Contention (Ara / AraXL ~220&ndash;340 kGE)</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="t-desc">
          Scales to 4&ndash;16 parallel execution lanes with 4096-bit vector registers. Multi-banked VRFs (8&ndash;16 banks per lane) and wide 128b&ndash;512b AXI4 crossbars prevent memory stalls, sustaining up to 16 FLOPS/cycle on dense GEMM.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Connector 2 -> 3 -->
      <div class="flow-connector">
        <span class="conn-icon">&darr;</span>
        <span class="conn-text">Transition from 1D Vector Registers to 2D Spatial Systolic Mesh (+240 kGE Density Expansion)</span>
      </div>

      <!-- Axis 3 -->
      <div class="triad-card bg-light-teal">
        <div class="triad-head">
          <span class="t-badge badge-teal">AXIS 3 &bull; 2D SPATIAL SYSTOLIC</span>
          <h4 class="t-title">Dedicated Spatial Matrix Acceleration (Gemmini ~580 kGE)</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="t-desc">
          Replaces 1D vector register files with a rigid 2D grid of 256 multiply-accumulate PEs. Delivers 354+ Ops/cycle on quantized neural network workloads via autonomous scratchpad DMA transfers and weight-stationary reuse.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 1.4 The 8-Archetype Architectural Spectrum (Top-Down Flow) -->
    <h3 class="subsection-title" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">1.4 The 8-Archetype Architectural Spectrum &amp; Phase Transitions</h3>
    <p class="sub-lead">
      SCORE establishes the first unified taxonomy organizing all open-source hardware accelerators into eight progressive microarchitectural archetypes across three computational domains:
    </p>

    <div class="spectrum-domain-wrap">
      <!-- Domain A -->
      <div class="domain-header">
        <span class="badge badge-yellow">DOMAIN A: 1D SUB-WORD &amp; VECTOR DATAPATHS</span>
        <span class="d-sub">Sequential vector register files with linear lane scaling</span>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 1</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Packed Sub-Word SIMD (Zero-VRF / DSP Extension)</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Slices existing 32-bit or 64-bit integer registers into 2&times;16-bit or 4&times;8-bit sub-words. Features zero architectural vector state; uses standard scalar pipeline ALU and GPRs for low-cost DSP (e.g. CV32E40P <code>pv.dotsp.b</code>, RISC-V P-Extension).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~15&ndash;30 kGE &bull; Peak: 4 MACs/cycle (INT8)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 2</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Compact Embedded Vector Coprocessor (Zve32 / Zve64)</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Ultra-compact standard RVV coprocessor (1&ndash;2 lanes, VLEN=128&ndash;512b) coupled to embedded scalar cores via low-latency dual-port interfaces. Prioritizes minimal area and latch-based VRFs for quantized edge ML (e.g. Spatz, Vicuna).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~35&ndash;65 kGE &bull; Peak: 1&ndash;2 Ops/cycle (FP64) / 8 Ops/cycle (INT8)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 3</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Standard Scalable Monolithic RVV 1.0 Coprocessor</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Full-profile vector coprocessor with multi-lane parallel SIMD execution (2&ndash;16 lanes), wide vector registers (VLEN=512&ndash;4096b), element chaining, and wide non-blocking AXI4 memory crossbars (e.g. Ara).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~180&ndash;300 kGE (4L) &bull; Peak: 8&ndash;32 Ops/cycle (FP64)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 4</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Clustered Multi-Bank VRF Scale-Up</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Deconstructs monolithic VRF crossbars into grouped lane sub-clusters with distributed local register banks and hierarchical interconnects, mitigating \(O(N^2)\) routing congestion in wide 8&ndash;16 lane configurations (e.g. AraXL).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~320&ndash;550 kGE (8L) &bull; Peak: 16&ndash;64 Ops/cycle (FP64)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Domain B -->
      <div class="domain-header" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <span class="badge badge-teal">DOMAIN B: DECOUPLED STREAMING &amp; SPATIAL MATRIX ACCELERATORS</span>
        <span class="d-sub">Eliminates scalar register fetch bottlenecks via autonomous dataflows</span>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 5</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Decoupled Access-Execute (DAE) Streaming Engine</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Decouples memory address generation from computing lanes. Autonomous AGUs prefetch operands directly into execution FIFOs via Stream Semantic Registers (SSR) and hardware repeat loops (FREP), eliminating scalar issue bubbles (e.g. Snitch Cluster, Hwacha).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~40&ndash;80 kGE/PE &bull; Peak: Sustained 100% Lane FPU Saturation</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 6</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">2D Spatial Systolic Matrix Engine</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Rigid 2D grid of multiply-accumulate processing elements (\(16	imes16\) to \(32	imes32\)) with spatial weight-stationary dataflow, autonomous DMA controllers, and dedicated double-buffered scratchpad SRAMs (e.g. Gemmini).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~500&ndash;1,200 kGE &bull; Peak: 256&ndash;1,024 Ops/cycle (INT8)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 7</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Vector-Matrix Hybrid (2D Tile Tensor Engine)</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Bridges 1D vector registers with 2D tile matrix accumulator registers (ZA tile registers), accelerating fused outer-product matrix multiplication directly within the processor ISA (e.g. RISC-V IME, T-Head Matrix Extension, TVM-VTA).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~400&ndash;850 kGE &bull; Peak: 64&ndash;256 Ops/cycle (BF16/FP16)</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Domain C -->
      <div class="domain-header" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
        <span class="badge badge-coral">DOMAIN C: MASSIVELY PARALLEL MANY-CORE SIMT</span>
        <span class="d-sub">Thread-level execution divergence and hardware warp scheduling</span>
      </div>

      <div class="archetype-step-card">
        <div class="arch-badge">ARCHETYPE 8</div>
        <div class="arch-info">
          <h4 class="arch-title">Many-Core SIMT (GPGPU Coprocessor)</h4>
          <p class="arch-desc">
            Multi-threaded SIMT execution engine with hardware warp schedulers, divergence stack managers, memory coalescers, and shared barrier scratchpads executing full OpenCL and CUDA compute kernels (e.g. Vortex GPGPU).
          </p>
          <div class="arch-meta">Silicon Footprint: ~1,500&ndash;3,500 kGE (4-Core) &bull; Peak: 16&ndash;64 Ops/cycle (FP32)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

