    <div class="ppa-breakdown-cards">
      <div class="ppa-card">
        <h4 class="ppa-card-title">1. The VRF Area Hegemony (45% to 58% of Core Silicon)</h4>
        <p class="ppa-card-desc">
          In wide vector machines (such as Ara 4096b VLEN), physical register storage accounts for over half the silicon gate budget. Using standard D-flip-flops inflates area catastrophically; production implementations utilize high-density single-port SRAM macros with multi-bank interleaving or specialized pulse latches.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="ppa-card">
        <h4 class="ppa-card-title">2. Quadratic Interconnect Explosion in Monolithic Crossbars</h4>
        <p class="ppa-card-desc">
          Scaling a monolithic vector coprocessor from 4 lanes to 16 lanes quadruples arithmetic units but increases crossbar routing multiplexers by \(16	imes\) (\(O(N^2)\)). AraXL circumvents this bottleneck by clustering lanes into autonomous 4-lane domains with hierarchical bridge routers, reclaiming 73% of crossbar area.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

