  <!-- SECTION 6: SENSITIVITY & THE UTILIZATION COLLAPSE DEEP-DIVE               -->
  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <section id="configuration-scaling" class="content-block">
    <div class="section-tag-wrap">
      <span class="badge badge-accent">SECTION 6</span>
      <span class="badge badge-yellow">EMPIRICAL COLLAPSE ANALYSIS</span>
    </div>
    <h2 class="block-title">6. Configuration Sensitivity &amp; The Vector Coprocessor Utilization Collapse</h2>
    <p class="block-lead">
      Our cycle-accurate Verilator benchmarking across standard scientific and neural network kernels exposes a fundamental vulnerability in modern tightly-coupled vector coprocessors: the catastrophic drop in hardware utilization when transitioning from regular matrix multiply to element-wise operations.
    </p>

    <div class="callout callout-warning">
      <div class="callout-header">
        <span class="badge badge-accent">CRITICAL EMPIRICAL FINDING</span>
        <h3 class="callout-title">The Vector Coprocessor Utilization Collapse</h3>
      </div>
      <p>
        In an ideal vector accelerator, every execution lane's FPU should fire every cycle (100% utilization). However, across our measured test suite on Ara and Spatz, hardware lane saturation plunges precipitously:
      </p>
      
      <div class="collapse-bars" style="margin: 1.25rem 0;">
        <div class="c-bar-item">
          <span class="c-lbl"><code>fmatmul</code> (Dense FP64 GEMM):</span>
          <div class="c-track"><div class="c-fill fill-green" style="width: 98.4%;">98.4% Peak Lane Utilization</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="c-bar-item">
          <span class="c-lbl"><code>fconv2d</code> (2D Image Convolution):</span>
          <div class="c-track"><div class="c-fill fill-green" style="width: 76.2%;">76.2%</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="c-bar-item">
          <span class="c-lbl"><code>fdotproduct</code> (Vector Dot Product):</span>
          <div class="c-track"><div class="c-fill fill-yellow" style="width: 61.5%;">61.5%</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="c-bar-item">
          <span class="c-lbl"><code>fft</code> (1024-Point Radix-2 Complex FFT):</span>
          <div class="c-track"><div class="c-fill fill-orange" style="width: 33.3%;">33.3%</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="c-bar-item">
          <span class="c-lbl"><code>softmax</code> (Element-Wise Reduction &amp; Exp):</span>
          <div class="c-track"><div class="c-fill fill-red" style="width: 23.0%;">23.0% (Utilization Collapse)</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="collapse-analysis-grid">
        <div class="col-analysis-card">
          <h4>Root Cause: Host Scalar Core Instruction-Issue Bottleneck</h4>
          <p>
            Ara and Spatz rely on an in-order host core (CVA6 or Snitch) to issue vector instructions. In <code>fmatmul</code>, each <code>vfmacc</code> instruction operates on wide vector registers (\(VLEN=4096\)), executing for 64 to 128 cycles per instruction. This gives the scalar core ample time to prepare subsequent addresses and loop counters without stalling.
          </p>
          <p>
            In contrast, during <code>softmax</code>, the algorithm executes rapid sequences of vector-scalar reductions (<code>vfredsum</code>), element-wise exponentiations, and scalar divisions. The vector lanes complete these short micro-operations in 2 to 4 cycles, while the scalar host requires 10 to 15 cycles to manage loop branches and scalar normalization. As a result, <strong>the vector lanes spend 77% of their time completely idle</strong> waiting for the scalar core to issue the next command.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="col-analysis-card">
          <h4>The Architectural Remedy: Decoupled Access-Execute (DAE &amp; SSR)</h4>
          <p>
            To solve the utilization collapse without resorting to power-hungry Out-of-Order speculative issue engines, advanced open-source architectures employ <strong>Decoupled Access-Execute (DAE)</strong>:
          </p>
          <ul style="margin: 0.5rem 0 0 1rem; font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.5;">
            <li><strong>Stream Semantic Registers (SSR):</strong> In the Snitch architecture, autonomous hardware address generation units (AGUs) stream memory operands directly into execution FIFOs mapped as register aliases (<code>ft0</code>). Instructions consume data as soon as it arrives, eliminating address-generation instructions entirely.</li>
            <li><strong>Hardware Repeat Loops (FREP):</strong> A single floating-point repeat instruction issues a loop body directly to the FPU sequencer for up to 256 iterations, completely decoupling the vector datapath from the scalar instruction fetch unit and restoring 100% compute lane saturation.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

